WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.820
One motor neuron loses many branches.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.160
Does it pull them all back together, on one schedule?

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.870
If the neuron ran one program for its whole arbor, its branches would retreat

4
00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:15.870
together.

5
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.710
In 2001, Keller-Peck, Lichtman and colleagues made a small, random subset of motor

6
00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:24.710
axons glow.

7
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.120
They traced every branch of single axons.

8
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.460
Each axon was losing branches.

9
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:36.563
Its branches withdrew at different times, at different junctions, without an obvious

10
00:00:36.563 --> 00:00:37.563
spatial pattern.

11
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:39.793
They did not go together.

12
00:00:40.613 --> 00:00:43.153
That points to a separate contest at every junction.

13
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:50.907
In 2003, Mark Walsh and Lichtman watched one of those contests.

14
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.450
Two motor axons carried different colours.

15
00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:58.850
The same living junction was imaged again and again, over days.

16
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.440
A fluorescent snake toxin marked the receptors.

17
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.820
The dose was small, so transmission still worked.

18
00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:11.093
Predict. Axon A starts with more of the receptor map.

19
00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:13.253
Does the bigger territory win?

20
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:24.787
Not reliably. The starting territory did not reliably predict the winner.

21
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.080
The share could shift one way, then back.

22
00:01:30.033 --> 00:01:33.413
Typically, one axon withdrew from the receptor sites.

23
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:36.233
The other extended into the sites it left.

24
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:46.467
So starting size alone does not reliably decide.

25
00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:51.587
Each junction runs its own contest, and what tips it is the next question.
